WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:01.650
Chris: Sally hold on, I got a call *phone
ringing*

00:00:01.650 --> 00:00:02.650
Chris: Chris Waters.

00:00:02.650 --> 00:00:04.220
John: Hey, how's it going man?

00:00:04.220 --> 00:00:05.359
Chris: Good John, how are you?

00:00:05.359 --> 00:00:07.470
J: I'm good, are we going to do this meet
in the middle thing?

00:00:07.470 --> 00:00:10.910
I think it's technically public property,
right, because it's on the creek?

00:00:10.910 --> 00:00:16.150
C: Technically public property located perhaps
behind the largest house in Indianapolis.

00:00:16.150 --> 00:00:21.090
J: I'll also bring Eugenia Price's Beauty
from Ashes if you bring a little, uh, a snack.

00:00:21.090 --> 00:00:24.030
C: Do you think Jimmy John's will deliver
if we give 'em the coordinates?

00:00:24.030 --> 00:00:25.030
J: *laughter*

00:00:25.030 --> 00:00:32.900
C: Alright, thank goodness I wore my speedo
today since we're going on the creek.

00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:33.900
Time to go swimmin'.

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:34.900
J: Good morning Hank, it's Tuesday.

00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:36.179
It's actually Saturday but it will be Tuesday
in the fullness of time.

00:00:36.179 --> 00:00:39.039
So Sarah's new show with PBS, The Art Assignment,
just premiered.

00:00:39.039 --> 00:00:42.101
What she does is she goes around the country
visiting with working artists, we get to see

00:00:42.101 --> 00:00:47.229
some of their work, some art historical context
for it and then they give us an art assignment.

00:00:47.229 --> 00:00:52.180
J: I have to pick a friend and then meet that
person at the exact geographical midpoint

00:00:52.180 --> 00:00:53.339
between the two of us.

00:00:53.339 --> 00:00:55.280
Now many of you will be wondering: How is
this art?

00:00:55.280 --> 00:00:57.519
Well, you should watch the first episode of
The Art Assignment.

00:00:57.519 --> 00:01:01.870
So anyway my best friend Chris and I chose
each other and now we have to make our way

00:01:01.870 --> 00:01:03.479
here by 2 o'clock.

00:01:03.479 --> 00:01:05.990
C: It is 1:03 PM.

00:01:05.990 --> 00:01:12.490
Is art just some guy going on a hike with
his dog to arbitrarily meet a friend that

00:01:12.490 --> 00:01:15.340
he set up an appointment with three hours
ago?

00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:17.759
There's a certain excitement to it, nervousness
about it.

00:01:17.759 --> 00:01:19.670
J: Not going to lie, I'm pretty nervous right
now.

00:01:19.670 --> 00:01:22.619
Hank, one of the things this assignment makes
me think about is public and private space.

00:01:22.619 --> 00:01:25.469
Like on this side of the fence, those trees
belong to someone.

00:01:25.469 --> 00:01:28.229
But on this side of the fence the trees belong
to everyone.

00:01:28.229 --> 00:01:29.229
That's odd.

00:01:29.229 --> 00:01:30.400
Ok we have reached Williams Creek.

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:31.710
C: Leaving the road.

00:01:31.710 --> 00:01:33.310
There's trusty Mya.

00:01:33.310 --> 00:01:37.010
J: The craziest thing about this is that I'm
right in the middle of the city of Indianapolis,

00:01:37.010 --> 00:01:40.280
a city of one million people, and, like, it's
just forest.

00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:41.780
Ah, this is intense.

00:01:41.780 --> 00:01:44.100
I'm gonna have to get down on my hands and
knees.

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:45.460
C: Not an easy hike.

00:01:45.460 --> 00:01:50.950
I've been on my hands and knees trying to
get through this.

00:01:50.950 --> 00:01:53.020
J: Ok, here we go.

00:01:53.020 --> 00:01:55.120
Hoo, down into the creek.

00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:56.460
*heavy breathing*

00:01:56.460 --> 00:02:00.159
C: Okay, comin' up on the last spot

00:02:00.159 --> 00:02:02.600
J: Honestly if I didn't have to meet Chris
at this point I would give up because the

00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:04.200
brush is so thick.

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:07.090
I don't want him sitting there, you know,
worrying about me or whatever.

00:02:07.090 --> 00:02:09.000
Wow, look at this, this is so beautiful.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:10.000
Wow.

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:12.600
Yeah, that's not something I would have seen
today.

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:15.060
I just heard a noise.

00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:17.120
Hup, oh I see him, I see him!

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:22.370
C: Some call him Jeremiah Johnson, some call
him Davy Crockett, but what he is is a frontiersman.

00:02:22.370 --> 00:02:25.430
And I think he's wearing his fancy Amsterdam
jacket.

00:02:25.430 --> 00:02:31.410
Like a spry jungle cat springing in to the
spring snow.

00:02:31.410 --> 00:02:32.410
Sic 'im, Mya.

00:02:32.410 --> 00:02:33.410
J: *breathing heavily* Oh, hey, Chris.

00:02:33.410 --> 00:02:34.410
C: Hey, John.

00:02:34.410 --> 00:02:35.410
J: How's it goin'?

00:02:35.410 --> 00:02:36.410
C: Fancy seeing you here

00:02:36.410 --> 00:02:37.622
J: Good to see ya.

00:02:37.622 --> 00:02:38.622
Come in for the real stuff.

00:02:38.622 --> 00:02:40.110
I'm so beat up, man.

00:02:40.110 --> 00:02:41.110
C: *chuckles*

00:02:41.110 --> 00:02:42.110
J: Ahh.

00:02:42.110 --> 00:02:43.430
C: Let me just look, let's look at that outfit.

00:02:43.430 --> 00:02:44.430
Let's look at-

00:02:44.430 --> 00:02:48.100
J: Aoh, my whole idea was to go as someone
who's really super professional, you know?

00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:49.660
Who has a...

00:02:49.660 --> 00:02:51.450
Like maybe a businessman who just got lost
in the woods.

00:02:51.450 --> 00:02:53.070
J: Ahh, it's just so beautiful, man.

00:02:53.070 --> 00:02:54.070
C: Yeah.

00:02:54.070 --> 00:02:55.590
J: It's such a cool thing that we wouldn't
have done today.

00:02:55.590 --> 00:02:56.590
C: No.

00:02:56.590 --> 00:02:57.590
J: You know?

00:02:57.590 --> 00:02:58.750
Like there's no way we would have ever done
this.

00:02:58.750 --> 00:02:59.750
C: No.

00:02:59.750 --> 00:03:00.750
J: Thanks for bringing the Jimmy John's.

00:03:00.750 --> 00:03:01.750
C: Jimmy John's.

00:03:01.750 --> 00:03:04.090
J: So I brought you a book actually, that
I thought you might enjoy.

00:03:04.090 --> 00:03:05.400
It's called, uh, Beauty from Ashes.

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:09.440
It's by a wo- a writer named Eugenia Price,
you ever heard of her?

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:12.860
C: No, I thought it was going to be an unnecessary
plug for The Fault in Our Stars.

00:03:12.860 --> 00:03:13.860
J: No, no.

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:14.860
It's just Beauty from Ashes.

00:03:14.860 --> 00:03:17.360
C: Oh, it sounds like a good book.

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:18.590
J: And it is a good book!

00:03:18.590 --> 00:03:22.010
C: It did make me think that this is how people
travelled-

00:03:22.010 --> 00:03:23.010
J: Mhm.

00:03:23.010 --> 00:03:24.060
C: -200 years ago.

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:27.650
J: And agreeing to meet at a certain time
and not having any way to contact each other

00:03:27.650 --> 00:03:28.660
if they can't, you know?

00:03:28.660 --> 00:03:30.330
C: I did feel like a kid again, though.

00:03:30.330 --> 00:03:31.330
J: Yeah.

00:03:31.330 --> 00:03:33.620
I haven't felt that kind of sense of adventure
since I was a kid.

00:03:33.620 --> 00:03:34.620
It's fun.

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:36.190
C: You need to get out more.

00:03:36.190 --> 00:03:37.620
J: Well, I don't know.

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:39.010
The internet's its own kind of adventure.

00:03:39.010 --> 00:03:42.120
So we've met in the middle, we've had our
lunch and now we're gonna walk out together,

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:43.120
isn't that romantic?

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:45.310
C: It was a delicious Jimmy Johns lunch sandwich-

00:03:45.310 --> 00:03:46.310
J: Oh, yeah.

00:03:46.310 --> 00:03:47.310
Thank you to Jimmy Johns.

00:03:47.310 --> 00:03:49.340
C: Thank you to Jimmy Johns and thank you
to John for bringing the, uh, wonderful novel

00:03:49.340 --> 00:03:50.340
that we shared.

00:03:50.340 --> 00:03:52.780
J: Hank, don't forget to be awesome, I'll
see you on Friday.

00:03:52.780 --> 00:03:55.840
C: Are you gonna post this as your video for
Tuesday?

00:03:55.840 --> 00:03:56.349
J: Yeah.

